<BaseLayout title="About the project & evidence methodology" description="How Glucose Atlas selects sources, separates evidence from interpretation, labels emerging research, and discloses its editorial limitations."><div class="page-shell"><span class="eyebrow">OUR APPROACH</span><h1 class="page-title">Clear explanations.<br/>Visible evidence. Honest limits.</h1><div class="prose" style="max-width:740px"><p>Glucose Atlas is an independent educational research project for curious readers, including engineers. It connects biological mechanisms, clinical measurements, treatment evidence and future research. It is not a healthcare provider.</p><h2>Editorial transparency</h2><p>This initial library was assembled with AI-assisted research and writing. Linked sources were checked on 30 September 2026. It has <strong>not been independently reviewed by a qualified medical professional</strong>. The displayed review date is a source-check date, not a clinical certification. Independent medical review is required before representing the project as a medically reviewed reference.</p><h2>How evidence is selected</h2><p>Clinical claims prioritize current ADA guidance and official NIH/NIDDK, CDC, WHO and FDA resources. Specific trial claims link to original reports when available. Regulatory claims specify the jurisdiction and indication. Trial registration is never treated as a positive result.</p><p>This is a curated narrative research library, not a registered systematic review. It does not claim exhaustive coverage of every trial or publication through 2026. Access years for living references are disclosed in source cards.</p><h2>Evidence labels describe confidence</h2>{evidenceLevels.map(level=><p><strong>{level}.</strong> {evidenceDescriptions[level]}</p>)}<h2>Study-design hierarchy</h2><ol><li>Systematic reviews, meta-analyses and major guidelines.</li><li>Randomized controlled trials.</li><li>Large prospective cohorts.</li><li>Observational studies.</li><li>Small human studies.</li><li>Mechanistic and preclinical research.</li><li>Expert opinion or consensus reasoning.</li><li>Hypothesis and speculation.</li></ol><p>A hierarchy is a starting point, not an automatic quality score. A meta-analysis of weak studies can remain uncertain. Consensus definitions can be useful without being randomized evidence. Population, outcome, bias, effect size and follow-up all matter.</p><h2>Research versus interpretation</h2><p>Source-linked findings describe the evidence. Analogy callouts explain a concept using another system. Conceptual diagrams contain no measured numerical predictions. Quantitative charts state units, populations and limitations directly beneath the plot.</p><h2>Privacy and search</h2><p>Search runs locally through Pagefind. There are no analytics trackers, external AI calls, accounts or submitted health data. The educational risk explorer holds selections only in memory. Color preference is stored in your browser. Search queries in the dedicated page URL may appear in normal browser history.</p><h2>Corrections and updates</h2><p>Content and references live in version-controlled MDX and typed data files. Changes require source verification and build checks. Outdated review dates and old sources are flagged for editorial review; older foundational studies are retained with context. Hosting this site does not automatically update its medical content.</p><h2>Medical context</h2><p>Use the library to prepare questions for a qualified clinician, not to diagnose yourself, calculate medication doses or stop treatment. Suspected emergencies require urgent local medical care. Guidelines and access differ between countries.</p><p><a href="/sources">Inspect the bibliography →</a> &nbsp; <a href="/report">Read the research in 18 parts →</a></p></div></div></BaseLayout>
